e3d296a15554ec.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/16f0eedeee65a71dd9d8fa83535d5f65bff44f1c424663fb2b393046c131e696.png)\n\nSimilarly, to calculate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/9b1bb30112995dd5d0f3e6cd147c26736878279484f82d4d73a086b8efdaec9a.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/27b99f04c6f0f2b24f2d107248a2315f6b2d2574f849bf47379d8f2fdd27d05e.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/b758dc5e36b9ce2daba82e94d58b5232cecff327cf85f3a2571675c5845ba90d.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/75c92641ef9ece42d3dab493a8e8fd78d276cd4f40e423f2b442d23464fb47c7.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/6ddd96143aa9754eb6b266d82b854a41fcd3e17d9d9c5aeec7d44a669e1994b3.png)\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/a1fb9fe4e3ce980bd3385ece84df7522afee0f98b5e57cf82e8c7e0a19a4119c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7862"] :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|D)P(D) + P(A|D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A|D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{11}+\\frac{12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7865"] :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D|A)P(A) + P(D|A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A) = 0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = 0.11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|A'),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.11 & =(0.08)(0.70)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110 & =0.056+0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.054 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
